  "summary": "Princess Crown is Atlus and Sega's action role-playing game, first released for Saturn in Japan on 11 December 1997.",
  "body": [
    "Princess Crown is Atlus and Sega's action role-playing game, first released for Saturn in Japan on 11 December 1997. Gradriel, the young queen of Valendia, leaves her castle to help her people personally and becomes involved in a struggle against the demon lord Lalva. Side-view roads connect towns and dangerous areas, with expressive two-dimensional characters giving the journey the appearance of an illustrated tale. Director George Kamitani built an adventure in which talking, preparing supplies and fighting occupy different parts of the same small fantasy world.",
    "Battles use direct attacks, dodges, shields and thrown weapons, with combinations produced through repeated inputs and movement. Each action consumes the Power gauge. Exhausting it leaves the character exposed, while walking or waiting allows it to recover. A successful exchange therefore includes a moment to withdraw as well as an opening to strike. Food and equipment support the journey between encounters: seeds can produce ingredients, and cooking turns those ingredients into more effective meals. Preparing something to eat becomes a practical part of surviving the next stretch of road.",
    "Completing Gradriel's adventure opens scenarios for other characters whose paths intersect with hers. Playing those journeys extends the story towards its true ending, allowing another perspective on people already encountered. The game remained a Japanese release, but its detailed sprites and theatrical side-view scenes became influential in Kamitani's later work. Princess Crown preceded the founding of Vanillaware and the creation of Odin Sphere. Its lasting identity comes from the relationship between that visual storytelling and the physical rhythm of combat, where even a powerful heroine needs time to regain her breath."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "The queen leaves the castle",
    "Taking a breath between attacks",
    "Stories beyond one heroine"
  ],
